--TEST--
`#[Json\Derive]` refuses a field typed at a `Core` value type that has no JSON form, at the property that declares it, instead of failing inside the decoder at run time — `rule:core-classes/derive-property-list`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:attributes/core-json-derive-and-core-json-field-a-class-with-a-json-codec
// `Core\Time\Instant` has a JSON form, so `$at` compiles.
// `Core\Time\Date` and `Core\Uuid` have none, so each property is an error.
#[Core\Json\Derive]
final class Event
{
    public function constructor(
        public Core\Time\Instant $at,
        public Core\Time\Date $on,
        public ?Core\Uuid $id = null,
    ) {}
}

var $event = Core\Json::decodeAs<Event>('{"at":"2024-03-01T12:00:00Z","on":"2024-03-01"}');
echo "unreachable ", Core\Json::encode($event), "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0756]: `Event::$on` is declared `Core\Time\Date`, which has no JSON representation
%A
error[E0756]: `Event::$id` is declared `Core\Uuid`, which has no JSON representation
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
